WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.670
When you look up at the night sky, what do
you see?

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.950
Maybe it looks like a random smattering of
dim lights.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.300
Or perhaps you see in the stars the shapes
that can predict your future.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.820
If you’re a nerd like me, then you see a
field of data and numbers that reflect the

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.550
quantitative reality of our universe.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:26.130
To our distant ancestors, unimpeded access
to the celestial sphere was almost like a

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.720
god-given right.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.040
In fact, when many of them gazed at the stars
— and the dark spots in between — they

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.250
did see messages left for them by their gods.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:44.510
What exactly were those messages and how have
our interpretations of them changed over time?

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:49.239
When I decided to study both astrophysics
and mythology in college, everyone assumed

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:52.149
I would be focusing on astrology or constellation
myths.

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:57.620
Well, joke’s on them because I can still
only reliably identify a single constellation

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:03.260
— *shout out to Orion* — but along the
way, I have developed a real appreciation

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.500
for the role that constellations and their
myths play in adding to our understanding

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.490
of the physical universe.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.730
Before we had GPS, weather balloons, and smartphones,
humans relied on constellations to keep track

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:20.220
of time, predict weather, preserve culture,
and navigate as they spread around the globe.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.930
They also used the stars to foretell the future
and commune with their gods, which seemed

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.930
much more reasonable at a time before humans
discovered physics.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.980
This practical and religious knowledge was
encoded in myths.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:38.070
The Ojibwe people native to the Great Lakes
region in the US tell a myth about the Fisher constellation.

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:43.660
Long ago, the world was covered in snow and
ice, and there were no birds to announce the

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.170
coming of spring.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.680
The Fisher was a great hunter, who went up
to skyland to free the birds and bring warmth

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.170
back to the Earth.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.231
When the sky people caught Fisher freeing
the birds, they started to chase him, but

00:01:55.231 --> 00:02:00.070
he escaped to the sky, where he was trapped
forever by an arrow through his tail.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.340
This Ojibwe myth serves multiple purposes.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.120
It explains the changing of the seasons, and
the constellation it’s attached to acts

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.360
as a reminder of spring’s return.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.349
No matter what they used the constellations
for, the fact that ancient humans used them

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:16.690
at all meant that people were studying the
sky.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:21.080
The brightest minds around the world took
meticulous notes about how the sky changed

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.140
over time, and without those early astrologers,
the field of astrophysics as we know it would

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.090
be drastically behind.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.160
I would probably lose my astronomer’s card
if I didn’t tell you this at some point,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.480
so here goes.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.250
Our planet is just one of many orbiting a
star, which is just one of billions orbiting

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:43.090
a galaxy, which is itself also just one of
billions in the vast universe.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.090
The points of light that we see in the sky
are distant stars, and despite what the myths

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.350
may tell us, their arrangement is nothing
more than a coincidence.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.650
Based on early written records and physical
artifacts, most historians agree that ancient

00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:00.670
Mesopotamians were among the first to observe
the motion of stars in the sky and assign

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.580
divine meaning to it.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.750
Because the sky was believed to be the home
of many gods, the shifting shapes in the inky

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.270
darkness were interpreted as omens.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.590
Like an omnipotent teacher scribbling on the
blackest of boards.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.700
Babylonian sky watchers in the 18th century
BCE were especially prolific in their study

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.970
of the heavens.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:24.290
The Babylonians and their neighboring Assyrians
also gave us many of the constellations we

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.110
use in the West today, like the Crab, the
Bull, and the Twins.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:32.520
These days, we call them Cancer, Taurus, and
Gemini.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.140
Ancient Mesopotamians even invented the zodiac,
the sequence of 12 or 13 constellations that

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.580
the sun appears to “pass through” over
the course of a year, though the sequence’s

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:48.090
name and its association with horoscopes were
added by the Greeks almost 2 millennia later.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.099
Originally, the zodiac was used as a way to
keep track of time.

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:57.140
The Mesopotamian constellation myths trickled
into Egypt over a few hundred years after

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:03.360
it was conquered by Persia and then Alexander
the Great in the 6th and 4th centuries BCE.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.070
These new myths merged easily with the Egyptians
existing beliefs, because they had already

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.890
been using the patterns of the night sky to
keep track of time for practical reasons,

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.080
most of them having to do with farming.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.560
When Sirius, the brightest star in the sky
appeared over the horizon, ancient Egyptians

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.940
knew that it was almost time for the Nile
River to have its annual flood, which would

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.900
nourish the soil for next year’s crops.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.880
The constellation mythology spread to Greece
by the 3rd century BCE, where they were called

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.400
“katasterismoi,” meaning “placing of
the stars” because they believed the constellations

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.700
were put in the sky purposefully by the gods
to teach them lessons.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.060
The Greeks’ religious fervor combined with
their taste for numbers produced astronomy:

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.030
the meticulous and mathematical observation
of the sky… so that we can know exactly

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.449
how the gods want us to live our lives.

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:58.180
Unfortunately Greek astronomers like Ptolemy
spent all that effort describing a system

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:03.180
that relied on a fundamentally flawed assumption:
they believed the Earth was the center of

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.370
the universe.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:09.540
That belief held as both astrology and astronomy
made their way through the Islamic world and

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:14.680
over to Europe, where geocentrism remained
popular until the heliocentric revelation

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:15.970
in the 1500s.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:21.690
Nowadays, around 70 million Americans claim
to read their horoscope every day, and nearly

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:26.260
a third believe that astrology is real or
vaguely scientific.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.510
As a scientist, I don’t love those stats,
but astrology in western countries is mostly

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:36.919
a novelty, an interest that people might use
to help them make small decisions.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:42.100
But different astrologies developed more-or-less
independently in China and India, each with

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.290
their own constellations and interpretations.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:48.360
Astrology is still popular in some parts of
the east.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:53.290
In China, star charts may be consulted to
determine compatibility in love, or to make

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.120
a big career decision.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:01.170
In India, astrology is used by many to dictate
decisions about medicine, marriage, careers,

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:05.120
business deals… often in a harmful, discriminatory
way.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.240
I’ve always thought it was kind of a shame
that conversations about constellations are

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.070
so often dominated by the Greeks, because
some of the most beautiful views of space

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.160
can only be seen properly from the southern
hemisphere.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.200
You can see the blurry Magellanic clouds and
the great dark rift cutting across the stream

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.370
of the Milky Way.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.950
And because the stars you can see depend on
your latitude, you can even see different

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.259
constellations, like the Southern Cross.

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.500
Celestial navigation in the southern hemisphere
is inherently more difficult than in the north

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.479
because there’s no south star that stays
put as the rest of the stars appear to twirl

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:38.590
around it.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:43.200
Instead, they have the Crux, a small group
of four stars.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.509
Despite this difficulty, Polynesian wayfarers
used the southern stars to travel around the

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:53.500
Pacific more than 1000 years before Europeans
traversed the oceans using their compasses.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:58.410
In the time of ancient Greece, before Polaris
was our north star, the Crux was visible from

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.360
more northern latitudes.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:06.080
But the Earth’s axis wobbled, and now the
Crux is a quintessentially southern constellation,

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:12.500
one featured on several southern countries’
flags like Brazil, Samoa, New Zealand, and

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.650
Australia.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:19.229
Some scholars believe that the astronomical
record in Australia goes back 65,000 years,

00:07:19.229 --> 00:07:21.870
but the timing is hotly debated.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:26.080
The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
People are spread between several hundred

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:32.070
different language groups with a wide variety
of spiritual beliefs and lore.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:36.360
Despite their many cultural differences, cave
paintings and other archeological evidence

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.370
show that many Aboriginal Australians were
using stars as calendars for more than 10,000

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.380
years before the Babylonians read their motion
as omens.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:49.740
And in a time before written language was
developed, the user manual for these celestial

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.110
timekeepers was passed down through oral stories
and other artforms.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:54.110
One constellation common to many of the aboriginal
tribes was the Emu, whose head was the dark

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.110
Coalsack nebula nestled next to the Southern
Cross.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.400
Re-record
One constellation common to many of the aboriginal

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:03.150
cultures is the Emu, whose head was the dark
Coalsack nebula nestled next to the Southern

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.150
Cross.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.025
The Emu’s legs stream out behind her, following
the path of the milky way.

00:08:07.025 --> 00:08:11.860
Some stories say this is because the Emu used
to have great wings that it used to fly across

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:13.210
the sky.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:17.759
The Emu constellation’s appearance in the
sky coincided with egg-laying season for the

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:20.150
more earth-bound emus.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.699
Those eggs are an important source of nutrients
for the aboriginal Australians, and so the

00:08:24.699 --> 00:08:28.539
constellation was a life-saving reminder.

00:08:28.539 --> 00:08:32.729
The Pleiades star cluster is known among many
groups as the Seven Sisters.

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:37.000
Though the details of the story vary across
the continent, the sisters are usually said

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.042
to be running from something, maybe a hawk
or an unwanted suitor.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.040
The Yamaji people in Western Australia used
to predict how heavy the seasonal rains would be 

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.008
based on how bright or faded the Sisters looked in the sky.

00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:53.010
To some aboriginal cultures, the stars in

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:56.780
the Western constellation Orion form a canoe.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.450
According to the Yolngu people in the Miwatj
region of north-eastern Australia, the three

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:06.390
stars that make up Orion’s belt represent
three brothers fishing in the canoe.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:11.050
When one of them got hungry enough to break
the law by eating a sacred fish, Walu the

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:14.860
Sun-woman lifted them to the sky in punishment.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:19.870
So ancient humans all over the world used
the sky to learn and teach traditions, to

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:22.000
navigate and keep time.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.870
Unfortunately, so many people today are prevented
from connecting to the stars in the same way

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:28.690
our ancestors did.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.830
According to the Dark Sky Association, a nonprofit
dedicated to ensuring everyone has access

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:39.610
to the night sky, about 80% of the world population
has a diminished view of the sky because

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:45.110
of light and air pollution.But losing access
to the stars doesn’t have to be an inevitable

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.810
casualty of scientific progress.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:52.570
Through policy and individual action, we can
reduce atmospheric pollution of all kinds

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:56.010
and have more of a say in what we see when
we look up.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:00.220
Maybe one day I’ll even be able to see more
than just a handful of stars from my NYC apartment.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:03.260
A girl can dream, can’t she?

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:06.760
In the meantime, there are plenty of apps
you can use to acquaint yourself with the

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:12.260
night sky and track constellations as they
spin above you, even if you can’t see them.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.760
Just knowing they’re there will connect
you to all the humans who have contemplated

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:16.810
the stars before you.

